schema.md mixes two kinds of content with opposite ownership rules. The
table is the project's: init seeds it and then never touches the file again,
correctly. The prose above it — three header lines, where the body starts —
is pidly's, and everywhere else pidly's prose lives in .pidly/README.md,
which is regenerated on every init and which check compares against what
would be generated now.

So schema.md is the one place pidly's own documentation sits in a file pidly
has promised not to correct. That is not theoretical: the seed said "Four
header lines" from the day desc: was dropped until it was noticed while
doing 17, and every tracker that ran init in between has the wrong statement
in a file no upgrade can fix.

Seed instead: a heading, one sentence saying what the table is for and
pointing at .pidly/README.md for the format, and the table.

Drop the created and updated rows from the seed too. They are pidly's
fields, valid whether or not the table declares them, so listing them is
documentation-by-copy in the file that cannot be updated — the same fault
one row down.

Keep it markdown and keep it a table. The declarations have three axes, awk
parses it with no dependency, and it renders where the repository is read.
A flat key: value list would have to invent syntax for the required column
and would buy no parsing that is not already written.

This also makes the file a program input rather than a document, which 25
needs: pidly fields has to read the declared set and its order out of it.

Existing trackers keep whatever prose they have — init still will not
rewrite an owned schema.md — so this only changes what new projects get,
which is why it is worth doing before there are many of them.

Done when the seeded schema.md is heading, pointer and table; the reference
covers what the prose used to; and init.sh still proves an owned schema.md
is left alone.

28 reopens part of this: it decides whether the schema stays a markdown
table at all. Stripping the prose is right either way — pidly's
documentation does not belong in a project-owned file whatever its shape —
so this ticket stands, but do not treat "keep it markdown and keep it a
table" above as settled. That paragraph argued against a flat key: value
list on the grounds that the table parses cheaply and renders; 28 argues
that a pattern in a cell collides with the delimiter, which is an argument
about the container that this ticket assumed away.
